const d={"prompt": "Rewrite section VII to separate what happened from whether the record shows severe or pervasive conduct. Keep my findings unchanged.", "replies": ["Rewriting “VII. Findings: Anti-Harassment Policy”. Violet's version will show in that section, to use or not.", "Redrafted “VII. Findings: Anti-Harassment Policy”. What's new is highlighted; Undo is on the section."], "note": "I split Section VII into two parts. 'What the Record Shows' sets out, remark by remark, what happened and how the accounts were weighed. 'Application of the Policy' then asks whether the established conduct is severe or pervasive, or affected the terms and conditions of employment. The determinations are unchanged: two remarks established and one not, which is partially substantiated on the component question, and not substantiated on the policy overall. I moved two kinds of material out of the 'holy day' sub-section and into the policy analysis: the point that the remark could reasonably be heard as referring to Leah's pending request, and the 'single remark, not addressed to her, not heard by her' weighing. Should the strongest-contrary-evidence paragraph stay as it is, or would you like it shorter?", "html": "<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">Allegation: Did Marcus Doyle's conduct violate the Anti-Harassment Policy?</p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">There is insufficient evidence to substantiate that, more likely than not, Marcus Doyle's conduct violated the Anti-Harassment Policy. The evidence establishes that Marcus made two remarks about Fridays, one of which referred to a \"holy day.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"1\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 1\">1</button></sup> The evidence does not establish the third remark attributed to him. In this Investigator's judgment, the remarks that are established do not meet the policy's definition of harassment.</p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The Anti-Harassment Policy provides that \"Harassment includes unwelcome conduct based on a protected characteristic that is sufficiently severe or pervasive to create an intimidating, hostile, or offensive work environment, or that affects the terms and conditions of employment.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"2\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 2\">2</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">The policy lists verbal harassment, including \"Offensive jokes, slurs, epithets, or name-calling,\" among the conduct prohibited when based on or related to protected characteristics.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"3\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 3\">3</button></sup> It lists religion as a protected characteristic.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"4\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 4\">4</button></sup></p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">Leah Goldberg, Member Services Representative II, alleges that Marcus, Team Supervisor, Member Services, made three remarks connected to her religious observance and the Q4 Friday rotation.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"5\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 5\">5</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">This Investigator first determines what each remark was and whether it was made.</span> <span class=\"rev-new\">Each remark is assessed separately, because evidence about one occasion does not corroborate another.</span> <span class=\"rev-new\">This Investigator then considers, under Application of the Policy, whether the established conduct is severe or pervasive.</span></p>\n<h3 class=\"text-lg font-semibold mt-6 mb-2.5 text-foreground leading-snug\">What the Record Shows</h3>\n<h4 class=\"text-base font-semibold mt-5 mb-2 text-foreground leading-snug\">\"We'll see about Fridays\"</h4>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The evidence establishes that Marcus said \"We'll see about Fridays\" at the September 9 huddle.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"6\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 6\">6</button></sup> Leah recalled the remark, Jordan Kim, Member Services Representative, recalled \"something like\" it, and Marcus said he \"Probably\" said it.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"7\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 7\">7</button></sup></p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The parties disagree about whether the remark was directed at Leah. Leah recalled that Marcus \"looked right at me\" and said that \"Everyone knew what he meant.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"8\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 8\">8</button></sup> Contrary to Leah's account, Marcus said the remark meant they would see how the rotation worked out and that \"It wasn't aimed at Leah.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"9\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 9\">9</button></sup> Jordan attended the huddle but did not describe where Marcus was looking, and he said he \"didn't think much of it then.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"10\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 10\">10</button></sup> No independent corroboration was available for Leah's account that Marcus looked at her.</p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">Leah may sincerely have understood the remark as directed at her, given that her request was pending at the time. <span class=\"rev-new\">The words themselves, however, do not refer to religion.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"11\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 11\">11</button></sup> The evidence does not show, more likely than not, that Marcus aimed them at her observance.</p>\n<h4 class=\"text-base font-semibold mt-5 mb-2 text-foreground leading-snug\">\"If everyone wanted their holy day off, nobody would work Fridays\"</h4>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The evidence establishes that, after the huddle, Marcus said words to the effect of \"If everyone wanted their holy day off, nobody would work Fridays.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"12\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 12\">12</button></sup> Jordan is the only witness who heard the remark.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"13\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 13\">13</button></sup> He said he heard it himself from about six feet away, while Marcus was at his desk \"talking to himself, kind of loud.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"14\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 14\">14</button></sup></p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\"><span class=\"rev-new\">Jordan's account has two weaknesses that this Investigator has weighed.</span> First, Jordan has a relationship to the outcome. He sits next to Leah, he offered her the shift swap, and he closed his interview by saying \"Leah's great.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"15\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 15\">15</button></sup> Second, Jordan discussed the remark with Leah afterwards.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"16\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 16\">16</button></sup> Leah's account of the remark is therefore hearsay and does not independently corroborate it. <span class=\"rev-new\">These weaknesses do not displace Jordan's account.</span> Marcus said, \"I might have said something like that.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"17\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 17\">17</button></sup> That partial admission corroborates the substance of what Jordan heard.</p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The remark refers to religious observance.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"18\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 18\">18</button></sup> Marcus explained that it concerned staffing in general and not Leah.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"19\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 19\">19</button></sup> Jordan's account is consistent with that explanation in two respects: he did not describe the remark as addressed to anyone, and he did not say it named Leah.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"20\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 20\">20</button></sup></p>\n<h4 class=\"text-base font-semibold mt-5 mb-2 text-foreground leading-snug\">\"Find a job that fits her religion\"</h4>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The evidence does not establish that Marcus said Leah should find a job that fits her religion. <span class=\"rev-new\">First, the only account of the remark is secondhand at two removes: Jordan said he heard it from Carla Rivera, Workforce Management Analyst, and did not hear it himself.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"21\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 21\">21</button></sup> Second, Carla, the claimed source, said she never heard Marcus say Leah should find another job.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"22\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 22\">22</button></sup> She also said she did not hear him say anything about Leah's religion, and that she told Jordan only that Marcus had been \"grumbling about the Friday requests.\"<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"23\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 23\">23</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">The record shows Carla is not in Marcus's reporting line.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"24\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 24\">24</button></sup> It shows no tie between her and either party that bears on this account. Third, Marcus denied the remark.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"25\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 25\">25</button></sup> Fourth, no source gives a date for it. Jordan may honestly have recounted what he understood Carla to say, but the evidence does not show that the remark was made.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-lg font-semibold mt-6 mb-2.5 text-foreground leading-snug\">Application of the Policy</h3>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\"><span class=\"rev-new\">As summarized above, the evidence establishes two remarks.</span> <span class=\"rev-new\">The first is an ambiguous remark about Fridays at the huddle, whose words do not refer to religion and which the evidence does not show was aimed at Leah's observance.</span> <span class=\"rev-new\">The second is a single remark referring to a \"holy day\" that Marcus made at his desk.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"26\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 26\">26</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">The question is whether that conduct was severe or pervasive enough to create an intimidating, hostile, or offensive work environment, or whether it affected the terms and conditions of Leah's employment.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"27\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 27\">27</button></sup></p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">The strongest evidence against this finding is the \"holy day\" remark. A supervisor made it about religious observance, on the same day he made the huddle remark, while Leah's accommodation request was pending.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"28\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 28\">28</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">It could reasonably be heard as referring to that request.</span></p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">In this Investigator's judgment, that evidence does not change the outcome. The remark was a general comment about Friday staffing, not a joke, slur, epithet or name directed at Leah.<sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"29\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 29\">29</button></sup> <span class=\"rev-new\">It occurred once, it was not addressed to her, and she learned of it only secondhand.</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"30\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 30\">30</button></sup> The record does not show remarks of this kind on other occasions. Whether the remark reflects on Marcus's handling of the accommodation request is addressed under the Religious Accommodation Policy. <span class=\"rev-new\">Taken together, the established remarks do not rise to conduct \"sufficiently severe or pervasive to create an intimidating, hostile, or offensive work environment.\"</span><sup class=\"cite-sup-wrap\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"cite-sup\" data-revision-citation=\"31\" data-keep aria-label=\"View citation 31\">31</button></sup> The record does not show that they affected the terms and conditions of Leah's employment.</p>\n<p class=\"mb-4 leading-[1.75] text-foreground/90\">For these reasons, there is not sufficient evidence from which to infer that, more likely than not, Marcus Doyle's conduct violated the Anti-Harassment Policy.</p>", "citations": {"1": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "2": {"c": "603870cab7", "s": 15, "e": 15}, "3": {"c": "603870cab7", "s": 29, "e": 33}, "4": {"c": "603870cab7", "s": 23, "e": 23}, "5": {"c": "a569a200b3", "s": 17, "e": 17}, "6": {"c": "a38274fbf3", "s": 71, "e": 71}, "7": {"c": "a38274fbf3", "s": 71, "e": 71}, "8": {"c": "a38274fbf3", "s": 71, "e": 71}, "9": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "10": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 47, "e": 47}, "11": {"c": "a38274fbf3", "s": 71, "e": 71}, "12": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "13": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "14": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "15": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 23, "e": 23}, "16": {"c": "a38274fbf3", "s": 79, "e": 79}, "17": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 63, "e": 63}, "18": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "19": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 63, "e": 63}, "20": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "21": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 63, "e": 63}, "22": {"c": "e5008deea5", "s": 15, "e": 15}, "23": {"c": "e5008deea5", "s": 15, "e": 15}, "24": {"c": "e5008deea5", "s": 5, "e": 5}, "25": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 71, "e": 71}, "26": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "27": {"c": "603870cab7", "s": 15, "e": 15}, "28": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "29": {"c": "666c851735", "s": 63, "e": 63}, "30": {"c": "9364405afe", "s": 55, "e": 55}, "31": {"c": "603870cab7", "s": 15, "e": 15}}, "quotesVerified": 21, "source": "INV-2026-0096 · in-place section rewrite with the report open · Oct 7 2026"};
